@*
    OdsMenu — the overflow / row-actions dropdown (Odyssey Design System · components/Menu).

    The more_vert row-actions menu used on every record row. A MudMenu driven by
    a flat Items list: set Divider for a separator, Header for a group label,
    Danger for a destructive (error-colored) action. The trigger is always a
    more_vert icon button named by AriaLabel.

    That trigger is MudMenu's OWN icon-button activator (Icon + AriaLabel), not a
    button placed in ActivatorContent (issue #255). MudMenu wraps a custom
    ActivatorContent in a <div tabindex="0" role="button" aria-expanded>, so a
    button inside it was a button nested in a button: two tab stops per menu (25
    extra on a 25-row table), and aria-expanded on the unnamed div instead of the
    focused control. The built-in activator is ONE real button carrying
    aria-haspopup, aria-expanded and the name, and MudMenu returns focus to it on
    close. The old Trigger parameter (a custom trigger element) had no callers and
    could only have been built the nested way, so it was removed; a select-style
    control with its own trigger belongs on OdsPopupMenu instead.

    An item marked Disabled is NOT rendered. An action a record cannot take is absent from its menu
    rather than dimmed with an explanation, and any divider or header the omission orphans is
    dropped with it, so a menu never opens onto a stray rule or a group label heading nothing.

    That replaced the earlier treatment, where such an item stayed in the focus order carrying a
    sibling note that said why it was unavailable. The note was the right answer to the wrong
    question: the reason belongs to the RECORD, not to one row of one menu that has to be opened to
    find it. Where the user needs to know why a capability is missing, the surface says so in its
    own copy — an OdsRecordSection notice or a helper line — which is reachable without opening
    anything and is announced where the writing would happen.
*@

<MudMenu Icon="@Icons.Material.Filled.MoreVert"
         Size="Size.Small"
         AriaLabel="@AriaLabel"
         AnchorOrigin="@Anchor"
         TransformOrigin="@Transform"
         Class="@Class">
    <ChildContent>
        @foreach (var item in VisibleItems)
        {
            @if (item.Divider)
            {
                <MudDivider />
            }
            else if (!string.IsNullOrEmpty(item.Header))
            {
                <MudText Typo="Typo.overline" Class="px-4 py-1 mud-text-secondary">@item.Header</MudText>
            }
            else
            {
                @* OdsMenuItem.Icon is a Material Icons ligature (DS convention),
                   not a MudBlazor SVG constant — render it via the icon font in
                   the item content rather than MudMenuItem's Icon (which would
                   leave an empty icon gutter). See ItemContent. *@
                <MudMenuItem OnClick="item.OnClick">
                    @ItemContent(item)
                </MudMenuItem>
            }
        }
    </ChildContent>
</MudMenu>

@code {
    /// <summary>The menu items — normal items, dividers, and group headers in order.</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public IReadOnlyList<OdsMenuItem> Items { get; set; } = [];

    /// <summary>Which edge the popover anchors to.</summary>
    [Parameter] public OdsAlign Align { get; set; } = OdsAlign.End;

    /// <summary>Accessible name for the icon-button trigger.</summary>
    [Parameter] public string AriaLabel { get; set; } = "Actions";

    [Parameter] public string? Class { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The item's own content — one definition, so the two item shells cannot drift.</summary>
    private RenderFragment ItemContent(OdsMenuItem item) =>
        @<span class="odc-menu-item @(item.Danger ? "danger" : null)">
            @if (!string.IsNullOrEmpty(item.Icon))
            {
                @* Ligature or literal glyph, the same split the design system's ActionMenu makes —
                   the terms "§" is a real menu icon, not a Material name. *@
                @if (IsLigature(item.Icon))
                {
                    <span class="material-icons odc-menu-item-ic" aria-hidden="true">@item.Icon</span>
                }
                else
                {
                    <span class="odc-menu-item-glyph" aria-hidden="true">@item.Icon</span>
                }
            }
            <span class="odc-menu-item-body">
                <span>@item.Label</span>
            </span>
            @if (!string.IsNullOrEmpty(item.TrailingIcon))
            {
                @* Right-aligned affordance icon, revealed on row hover/focus
                   (Odyssey Design System · ActionMenu trailingIcon). *@
                <span class="material-icons odc-menu-item-trail" aria-hidden="true">@item.TrailingIcon</span>
            }
        </span>;

    /// <summary>
    /// The items actually drawn: every unavailable action removed, then every divider and header the
    /// removal orphaned.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// <para>
    /// A divider is orphaned when it would lead the menu, trail it, or follow another divider, or
    /// when the next item is a header (which brings its own separation). A header is orphaned when
    /// no real item follows it <b>before the next header</b> — a group label with nothing under it.
    /// Both passes run over the list that REMAINS, not the original, so two adjacent removals
    /// collapse the divider between them rather than each looking past the other.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// The header lookahead is bounded by <c>TakeWhile</c> rather than scanning to the end of the
    /// list, and that bound is load-bearing: with two adjacent groups whose FIRST is emptied by the
    /// removal, an unbounded scan finds a surviving item under the SECOND group and keeps the first
    /// header — leaving exactly the "group label heading nothing" this pass exists to remove. The
    /// design system's own reference implementation (<c>components/Menu.jsx</c>) scans unbounded and
    /// has the same gap; this is a deliberate divergence toward what its prose specifies, not a
    /// transcription error to be "corrected" back.
    /// </para>
    /// </remarks>
    private IReadOnlyList<OdsMenuItem> VisibleItems
    {
        get
        {
            var shown = Items.Where(i => !i.Disabled).ToList();
            return shown
                .Where((item, i) =>
                    item.Divider
                        ? i > 0 && i < shown.Count - 1
                          && !shown[i - 1].Divider
                          && string.IsNullOrEmpty(shown[i + 1].Header)
                        : !string.IsNullOrEmpty(item.Header)
                            ? shown.Skip(i + 1)
                                   .TakeWhile(n => string.IsNullOrEmpty(n.Header))
                                   .Any(n => !n.Divider)
                            : true)
                .ToList();
        }
    }

    /// <summary>A Material Icons ligature is all-lowercase ASCII letters, digits and underscores
    /// (e.g. "attach_file"); anything else (e.g. "§") renders as a literal glyph.</summary>
    private static bool IsLigature(string value) =>
        value.All(c => char.IsAsciiLetterLower(c) || char.IsAsciiDigit(c) || c == '_');

    private Origin Anchor => Align == OdsAlign.Start ? Origin.BottomLeft : Origin.BottomRight;
    private Origin Transform => Align == OdsAlign.Start ? Origin.TopLeft : Origin.TopRight;
}
